# Snap Interfaces for IoT
Common snap interfaces used in Ubuntu Core and IoT snap development.

## What are interfaces?
Interfaces allow snaps to access system resources or communicate with other
snaps. A plug (requester) connects to a slot (provider). Most slots are
provided by the system (snapd). Some are provided by other snaps.

Declaration format in snapcraft.yaml:
```yaml
plugs:
  network:          # auto-connects
  raw-usb:          # requires store review or --devmode
  serial-port:
    interface: serial-port
    path: /dev/ttyS0
```

## Networking

network             Outbound network access (TCP/UDP).
network-bind        Listen on network ports.
network-observe     Read network state (/proc/net, netlink).
network-control     Full network configuration (ip, iptables, etc.).
firewall-control    Manage firewall rules.

## Hardware access

raw-usb             Direct USB device access via libusb.
serial-port         Access a serial port device node.
gpio                Access a specific GPIO pin by number.
gpio-memory         /dev/gpiomem (Raspberry Pi style GPIO).
i2c                 Access an I2C bus device.
spi                 Access an SPI device.
can-bus             Access a CAN bus network interface.
hidraw              Access HID raw input devices.
hardware-observe    Read hardware information (/sys/class, /proc/bus, etc.).

## System access

system-files        Access arbitrary paths on the host filesystem.
                    Requires store assertion or --devmode.
home                Access the user's home directory.
removable-media     Access /media, /mnt, /run/media.
mount-observe       Read /proc/self/mountinfo.
disk-quota-observe  Observe disk quota information.

## Process / boot

process-control     Manage processes (kill, nice, ionice).
system-observe      Read system information (/proc, /sys).
shutdown            Initiate system shutdown or reboot.
snapd-control       Control snapd itself — install, remove, configure snaps.
                    Very privileged; requires store review.

## Logging / audit

log-observe         Read system logs (/var/log/syslog, journald).
journal-observe     Subscribe to systemd journal.

## Security

tpm                 Access the TPM device (/dev/tpm0, /dev/tpmrm0).
opengl              Access GPU hardware (Mali, VideoCore, etc.).
fwupd               Access fwupd for firmware updates.

## Common pitfalls

- Interfaces not listed here as "auto-connects" require manual connection
  with: sudo snap connect <snap>:<plug> <provider>:<slot>
- Check current connections: snap connections <snap-name>
- Use --devmode during development to skip interface confinement checks
- Store review is required for interfaces with broad system access before
  publishing to stable; use --dangerous locally to skip this

